<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class="wp-block-heading">5. Electronics safety warning</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Electronics practical work can involve electric shock, short circuits, overheating, damaged batteries, rotating machinery, hot soldering irons, sharp tools, lasers, chemicals and fire risk. By following any project guide you accept responsibility for your own workspace, construction, testing and supervision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->
<!-- wp:list {"className":"cs-checklist"} --><ul class="wp-block-list cs-checklist"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Check voltage, current, polarity, power rating, pin configuration and datasheet absolute limits before applying power.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Start with a current-limited supply where possible and remove power immediately if smell, smoke, unusual heat or sound occurs.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Handle Li-ion/Li-Po and lead-acid batteries only with correct chargers, protection and charging supervision.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Never treat a low-voltage tutorial as permission to work on 230 V mains or inside a powered appliance.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Do mains, inverter, motor-drive, medical, automotive or other high-risk work only under a qualified supervisor and applicable safety rules.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Use insulation, fusing, proper connectors, eye protection and a clean, dry work area.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->
<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you are unsure whether a circuit is safe, stop and ask a qualified laboratory instructor or professional. Do not power an uncertain circuit merely because the same diagram worked in a simulation or someone else’s build.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->

